module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.EtaleBasics.NormalCrossings.StrictHenselizationStalkIdeal

/-!
# Recognizing a strict normal crossings divisor by its strict henselizations

Let `X` be a locally Noetherian scheme and `D` a closed subscheme with strict normal crossings at
the strict henselizations — condition (2) of Tag 0CBS. Suppose in addition that at every point `p`
of `D` the local equation of `D` already factors in `𝒪_{X, p}` as a product `f₁ ⋯ f_k` with
`k ≥ 1` and each quotient `𝒪_{X, p} / (fⱼ)` regular. Then `D` is a strict normal crossings
divisor.

This is the local core of the implication (2) ⇒ (1) of Tag 0CBS. In the source the factorization
hypothesis is produced on an étale chart: a regular system of parameters of `𝒪^{sh}_{X, p}` is
realized by global functions `f₁, …, f_d` on an étale neighbourhood `(U, u)` of `p`, the divisors
`V(fᵢ)` are regular at `u` because their strict henselizations are the regular rings
`𝒪^{sh}_{X, p} / (fᵢ)`, and the J-2 hypothesis makes the regular locus open, so after shrinking
`U` Zariski-locally the factorization holds at *every* point of the chart. That chart construction
is `Stacks.Divisors.exists_etale_chart_forall_normal_factorization`, and feeding it to the
étale-chart form of the criterion below yields the equivalence of Tag 0CBS,
`Stacks.Divisors.isNormalCrossingsDivisor_iff_isStrictNormalCrossingsAtStrictHenselizations`.
The criterion below is the local step, which needs no J-2 or Nagata hypothesis.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Divisors.isStrictNormalCrossingsDivisor_of_exists_regular_factorization`: a closed
  subscheme with strict normal crossings at the strict henselizations whose local equations factor
  with regular factors is a strict normal crossings divisor.

## Implementation notes

The factorization hypothesis is stated on the stalk ideal rather than on sections over an open,
and the factors are quantified at every point of the support rather than on one chart; that is
what makes the statement local and keeps it free of any shrinking argument.

Given the factorization, the extension of the stalk ideal to a strict henselization `Ash` is both
`(f₁ ⋯ f_k) Ash` and the ideal generated by the product of an initial segment of a regular system
of parameters of `Ash`. Unique factorization in the regular local ring `Ash`, in the form of
`IsRegularLocalRing.isRegularLocalRing_and_exists_isSystemOfParameters_append_of_isHenselization`,
then identifies `k` with the length of that initial segment and exhibits `f₁, …, f_k` as the
initial segment of a regular system of parameters of `𝒪_{X, p}` — which is the strict normal
crossings condition at `p`, after rewriting the kernel of the stalk map of the closed immersion as
the stalk ideal.

The effective Cartier conjunct is supplied by
`Stacks.Divisors.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isStrictNormalCrossingsAtStrictHenselizations`, so
the factorization hypothesis is used only for the pointwise conjunct.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CBS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CBS)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0BI9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BI9)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory IsLocalRing

universe u

namespace Stacks.Divisors

/-- Let `D` be a closed subscheme of a locally Noetherian scheme `X` which has strict normal
crossings at the strict henselizations, and suppose that at every point `p` of `D` the stalk ideal
is generated by a product `f₁ ⋯ f_k` of at least one element with each quotient
`𝒪_{X, p} / (fⱼ)` a regular local ring. Then `D` is a strict normal crossings divisor.

In Tag 0CBS the factorization hypothesis is what the J-2 or Nagata assumption produces, by
realizing a regular system of parameters of `𝒪^{sh}_{X, p}` on an étale neighbourhood and
shrinking it so that each of the resulting divisors is regular. -/
@[stacks 0CBS "(2) ⇒ (1), the step that upgrades condition (2) to strict normal crossings once
each local equation factors into regular factors. The construction of an étale chart on which it
does, which is where the J-2 or Nagata hypothesis of the lemma enters, is not asserted."]
theorem isStrictNormalCrossingsDivisor_of_exists_regular_factorization {X : Scheme.{u}}
    [IsLocallyNoetherian X] (D : X.IdealSheafData)
    (hD : IsStrictNormalCrossingsAtStrictHenselizations D)
    (hfac : ∀ x : X, x ∈ D.support → ∃ fs : List (X.presheaf.stalk x),
      (∀ f ∈ fs, IsRegularLocalRing ((X.presheaf.stalk x : Type u) ⧸ Ideal.span {f})) ∧
        1 ≤ fs.length ∧
        AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheafData.stalk D x = Ideal.span {fs.prod}) :
    IsStrictNormalCrossingsDivisor D := by
  refine ⟨isEffectiveCartierDivisor_of_isStrictNormalCrossingsAtStrictHenselizations D hD,
    fun y ↦ ?_⟩
  have hx : D.subschemeι y ∈ D.support :=
    (Scheme.IdealSheafData.range_subschemeι (I := D)).le ⟨y, rfl⟩
  obtain ⟨fs, hfsreg, hfs1, hfsgen⟩ := hfac (D.subschemeι y) hx
  obtain ⟨Ash, _, _, _, _⟩ := IsLocalRing.exists_isHenselization_separableClosure
    (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y) : Type u)
  have : IsNoetherianRing Ash :=
    IsLocalRing.isNoetherianRing_of_isHenselization
      (A := (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y) : Type u))
      (SeparableClosure (ResidueField (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y))))
  have : IsLocalHom (algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y)) Ash) :=
    IsLocalRing.IsHenselization.isLocalHom
      (L := SeparableClosure (ResidueField (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y))))
  obtain ⟨hreg, hs, s, hsop, hsgen, -, hsle, hmap⟩ :=
    exists_isSystemOfParameters_map_stalk_of_comap_fromSpecStalk D (D.subschemeι y) hx
      (hD (D.subschemeι y) ⟨y, rfl⟩
        (SeparableClosure (ResidueField (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y)))) Ash)
  have := hreg
  -- Unique factorization in `Ash` matches the given factors against the initial segment.
  obtain ⟨hA, -, ts, htsop, htsgen⟩ :=
    IsRegularLocalRing.isRegularLocalRing_and_exists_isSystemOfParameters_append_of_isHenselization
      (SeparableClosure (ResidueField (X.presheaf.stalk (D.subschemeι y))))
      hfsreg hsop hsgen hsle (by rw [← hfsgen]; exact hmap)
  refine ⟨hA, fs ++ ts, fs.length, htsop, htsgen, hfs1, by simp, ?_⟩
  obtain ⟨U, hxU⟩ := exists_affineOpens_mem (D.subschemeι y)
  have hstalk := AlgebraicGeometry.stalk_ker_eq_of_affine_target D.subschemeι y U.1 U.2 hxU
  rw [Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_subschemeι] at hstalk
  rw [List.take_left, ← hstalk, hfsgen]

end Stacks.Divisors

end
